--- event netd.hostname.changed
tier: essential
gating: none — every change netd makes to the kernel's hostname is recorded

netd changed the machine's hostname. The name is what other machines, log
lines and certificate requests know the machine by, so a change of it is
always recorded.

netd takes the name from `Machine\System\Network Hostname`, or else from a
lease that offers one where the interface's profile accepts it, and
passes it to the kernel. A call that leaves the kernel's name as it was,
as when netd restarts on a machine that already has the name, records
nothing. A name the kernel refuses is not a change and is not recorded.

field: config.name                      required
  Always `Hostname`, the setting the name is held in, whether the value in
  force came from the registry or from a lease.
field: config.text                      required
  The name now in force, as netd passed it to the kernel.
field: config.text-previous             optional
  The kernel's name just before the change, as the kernel held it. On the
  first change after boot this is the kernel's own `(none)`. Absent when it
  could not be read.
field: subject.token.sid                optional
  The user of the token whose `reconcile` request on netd's control socket
  made the pass that changed the name. Absent when netd changed it on its
  own account: after the registry value changed, when a lease arrived, and
  at startup. Who wrote the registry value is LCS's to record, where the
  key's SACL audits the write.
